## Decisions

### Should CAFE Jayvens be visible to its current members, or to the whole school community?

I reproduced Gayle’s report on production for **CAFE Jayvens, November 6, 8–10am**. Ian’s admin view opens it; spoofing as **Bert Chan**, a Ravens parent, shows **“Not here.”** Bert already belongs to both Jayvens Parents and CAFE Jayvens. Spoofing correctly applies his permissions.

The cause is the activity’s **visibility setting**: `GROUP.visible_to` names the group whose effective members may read the activity. It is currently blank, which keeps this activity to managers and admins. Effective membership means direct members plus people selected by its rules. Its existing rules take Jays and Ravens members and their households, including siblings. The parallel invitation investigation found 11 extra siblings already sent invitations.

I’m asking because choosing who may see a currently hidden production item is an audience policy decision; your task instructions explicitly require that decision from you. No production data or code has changed, and Spoof Mode is restored to Ian.

- (*) Current activity members
  Set the visibility group to CAFE Jayvens itself, using the existing general membership rule. Bert Chan can open it. Jasmine Bicoy, a Grade 7 sibling already included through her Ravens household, can also open it while that membership remains. A parent outside the activity’s membership cannot open it. Managers and admins retain access. This is my recommendation because visibility follows the group’s actual membership; it requires one production field update and no special-case code.
- ( ) Whole school community
  Set the visibility group to Everyone, the existing group containing the school community. Bert Chan and Jasmine Bicoy can open it, and so can a parent outside the activity’s membership. Managers and admins retain access. This makes the page available beyond its current guest membership.

